IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Writ Petition (Civil) No. 562 of 2012
CITATION: (2019) 3 SCC 39
YEAR: 2019

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: Dipak Misra, R.F. Nariman, A.M. Khanwilkar, D.Y. Chandrachud, Indu Malhotra

PARTIES:
Joseph Shine vs Union of India

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Indian Penal Code, 1860, Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973, Constitution of India

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 497 IPC, Section 198(2) CrPC, Article 14, Article 21

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
decriminalization of adultery, gender equality, patriarchy struck down, dignity of women

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Struck down Section 497 IPC criminalizing adultery as unconstitutional, ruling that treat women as chattel of their husbands violates Articles 14 and 21.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 497 IPC, Section 198(2) CrPC, Article 14, Article 21.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

